#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192337 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "00b0d838-163d-5299-beaf-255f9b13cf71" :question/math-academy-id "e-11034" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, x^2+y^2 \\geq 4 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A - \\partial A = \\textrm{int}\\, A$\n\n- $\\textrm{int}\\, A \\cap \\partial A = \\emptyset$\n\n- $A = \\textrm{int}\\,A \\cup \\partial A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $\\partial A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/2c7e7ac1b320591250b045ebe493b28e0bb9cdb38469324ee496aacb8766c75b.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\textrm{int}\\, A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = A - \\partial A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/49ffc47c2ae1cde5e93e8607db8ef6b58eb7938913f82ea41f281df1fe334c14.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\textrm{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A - \\partial A = \\textrm{int}\\, A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\textrm{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\textrm{int}\\, A \\cap \\partial A = \\emptyset,$ where $\\emptyset$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $A = \\textrm{int}\\,A \\cup \\partial A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192338 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "a8045dd8-90b8-5671-9003-2539c502cb49" :question/math-academy-id "e-11035" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\,  y^2 - 1 \\leq  x , \\, -2 \\leq y \\leq  2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}, \\, -2 <     y <     2\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $\\partial A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/1ca3afd22a68f2ce8b29645879967c3d130d5c19a7d529296ff129eb92693295.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\textrm{int}\\, A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = A - \\partial A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/6e7f7a326a89c60bbc11e48d491ed05a176161881368f189aec7aad916752b64.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{y^2 - 1 < x}, \\, -2 <     y <     2\\big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192356 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e534c006-a5b4-5f76-bbd6-451496358d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Boundary of a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "44971e25-0367-5b13-a6be-db503c6b4539" :question/math-academy-id "e-12399" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in\\mathbb R^2 : x^2+y^2 -1<0\\}\n$$\n\nThe boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\partial A = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,: \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}} \\, \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $\\partial A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/8d3c7549948f0dd0f6a634526f1d978ae69d0c5b25611981b63a7cf389608087.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the circle $x^2+y^2 = 1.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\partial A = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{x^2+y^2 = 1} \\, \\big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7bde2ac-13ae-5cb4-8a46-28e6a102274d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312817} {:answer/id #uuid "f6aac1d9-0fab-506e-b452-870bf182ae56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312818} {:answer/id #uuid "17b24f0a-ea4d-56b6-a160-1f892e94b3ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312819} {:answer/id #uuid "9680077f-91e6-587c-9def-0e476be40c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312820} {:answer/id #uuid "683016d7-8f07-57a7-b83b-63c2a724c3fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2<x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "683016d7-8f07-57a7-b83b-63c2a724c3fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2<x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312821} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7d36af1-f5d0-5d6c-971b-53bfe8da33f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3430876-1457-5775-9fe8-70276e1b4953" :question/math-academy-id "q-105176" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2\\le x<0,\\,0<y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,,\\,0<y<1\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/4f65a735e53130f633635fdfc8cd6de6c4fa39ed1fb7913ba9679bb07e73a6a0.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/c76cea9b33420bf6c2e03d8eecb105003ce8b4e619063cbf2b7fccd5bc596cb2.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2<x<0,\\,0<y<1\\,}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e534c006-a5b4-5f76-bbd6-451496358d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Boundary of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "819ad07f-30a8-560a-823e-cc39b532b055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} > 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190916} {:answer/id #uuid "8edb4482-ff3c-5a51-9cfe-12eae815fd3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} < 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190917} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4b5bf9-83e1-55b2-86b2-a9505998428b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} \\le 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190918} {:answer/id #uuid "000e1228-4f30-5e96-8cb2-f92e6a06377b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} \\ge 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190919} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6800af-f876-5f20-8ac9-78af6242f8a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e6800af-f876-5f20-8ac9-78af6242f8a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190920} :answer-field/id #uuid "21bf9d21-1513-55c7-8d72-9cd054a73e71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9afd51d6-5b72-58c9-b163-8fcc7c81ce98" :question/math-academy-id "q-158313" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/c2452160f8af4651bdc78756b7c0df8402de185ba7a7bedbc1c1a676b342af97.png)\n\nConsider the set $A,$ shown above. The boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/eea68ab4e4b440609fcc4f6c2d40c4dc9fc394fc45f1047ec9c658729322749a.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the parabola ${y}^{2}=4x.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{y}^{2}=4x\\,}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89065094-1124-5c4c-b1ba-c19584e623f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0 < y < 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190922} {:answer/id #uuid "c816bd8f-37c2-5162-aeab-cc70b2a140d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190923} {:answer/id #uuid "0a26dc4f-08a6-5c47-b9e4-8d674fe40cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1 < y < 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190924} {:answer/id #uuid "364d78c9-1933-54db-af3c-1aebdf1418b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190925} {:answer/id #uuid "765c7aa5-d5d4-57d2-9fa7-98220be62120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1 \\le y \\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a26dc4f-08a6-5c47-b9e4-8d674fe40cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1 < y < 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190924} :answer-field/id #uuid "500766af-f20f-5af3-8f07-21325ccfaf03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "846d6059-ad4f-5e01-8a65-610c7d23e3a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-158314" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/b95c538613ee8672e0f1cac970b7af230ec40bf3075e609b1e03f7cad4382fb1.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/19e8e52881066864cba562ed2a8cdc2944b9dae416c7124fdee207cdad1d8bdf.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/907e996fb7aea36e0c924feb0f5d9dc6804c768be775953d277e84b0145ef5a0.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,-1<y<0}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ce0f030-b4c1-57a9-9722-3cd31e13688f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191084} {:answer/id #uuid "e87713fa-7bfd-5c4e-a4f4-e7924ad8d147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191085} {:answer/id #uuid "031a8edc-724f-5134-b433-606b44e5f4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191086} {:answer/id #uuid "17616b13-a46d-58b0-bc4e-cc01d95576bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191087} {:answer/id #uuid "459ba8c5-ae94-5c84-afdf-ba80e4ecdae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "031a8edc-724f-5134-b433-606b44e5f4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191086} :answer-field/id #uuid "e55d3b07-e262-5483-8899-20d53781acb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "68256c62-7faf-5747-9e8c-b2c67a0a95a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161444" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/62c410c21e79e1bc688c1ff1a3440a207c62bcad42525bdc93fa2770b76d48bd.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/33a4738b642443c2535d347aa53ee2b7b80c800142c4844d94578769118ebfbc.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e534c006-a5b4-5f76-bbd6-451496358d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Boundary of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cb2982c-07d6-5470-b21e-fbea1fb4efc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312824} {:answer/id #uuid "7772eccd-6e38-5abf-a54e-8586c460ef18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312825} {:answer/id #uuid "18a83002-9b42-5830-9e58-50acf6cd7024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\ge 16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312826} {:answer/id #uuid "55ad9afd-b849-5211-811f-e604a7159a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}>16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312827} {:answer/id #uuid "f60dd168-6989-578f-ac25-6627ee5af362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cb2982c-07d6-5470-b21e-fbea1fb4efc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312824} :answer-field/id #uuid "959c2d3c-c8fc-5ee1-aca0-44e95cc4a1d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27e84be7-f3dd-5b1a-a260-43fabfe78370" :question/math-academy-id "q-161590" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 16}.\n$$\n\nThe boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/b1074330fc650cf6af682f173f50943d779819afa59bf5be19edb88def1ef03a.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=16.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=16\\,}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e534c006-a5b4-5f76-bbd6-451496358d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Boundary of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8840e161-f220-5fc7-a2f1-72b04d8b7dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>-1+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312831} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd290f4-ff15-5b5d-8f49-c79fd5e6a0f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<-1+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312832} {:answer/id #uuid "1f458d34-b653-5d04-82fb-8c4aef6f739a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312833} {:answer/id #uuid "2526c215-dd8a-53f3-bee1-16e3103c85b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge -1+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312834} {:answer/id #uuid "0effa21f-90ea-50c4-80f7-f79242509fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le -1+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f458d34-b653-5d04-82fb-8c4aef6f739a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312833} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9f073f4-91eb-5bd4-9d7f-6b4f2de76179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a26d47e-204e-5e18-9063-2683b6b0394d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161593" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/80bde6a20ceed7c052a87eda6f05c542f6046de7014f6402f5f453b6610f8d42.png)\n\nConsider the set $A,$ shown above. The boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/05b25521109f373e2a7718523ce6289b27b37bd185d4fc60f0d052c232f4f8a4.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the line $y=-1+x.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y=-1+x\\,}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e534c006-a5b4-5f76-bbd6-451496358d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Boundary of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244a0dd3-1c2f-5bd6-9762-8c20ce0cf886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312838} {:answer/id #uuid "a27ee62b-a7a9-5a02-afe8-c855edc4adbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}>36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312839} {:answer/id #uuid "aae007dc-f538-5498-80ec-fe74d572d1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312840} {:answer/id #uuid "d24298c2-bc97-5830-a89b-d8d2b4de4308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312841} {:answer/id #uuid "0da20030-e113-5726-9bb4-36b205b8e036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\ge 36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aae007dc-f538-5498-80ec-fe74d572d1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312840} :answer-field/id #uuid "02d7cd99-5e48-55ff-b82d-4b505d9773bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bcfdd46f-14ab-5535-a276-fcd0db509adc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161598" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/112ab676fa5a97db198a872c33579900deab76e0c3c9c92016e165a67482bbcd.png)\n\nConsider the set $A,$ shown above. The boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/44f3a2a8e535c7248dac49d126da9bf17fce94c357f15c93a3eaad665e73b47a.png)\n\n \n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.$  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36\\,}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e534c006-a5b4-5f76-bbd6-451496358d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Boundary of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e067a2d-a6f5-52ff-b110-b65c2fa82a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312845} {:answer/id #uuid "ce412e86-0c45-5e9f-9ab5-f337efcf6dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y<{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312846} {:answer/id #uuid "7f764a67-5694-5182-896d-ef7b4c967bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y>{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312847} {:answer/id #uuid "cda9f910-7acf-595d-a614-b3cf5d1ee5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y\\le {x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312848} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4ee260-3bc9-5400-bf56-47d9322bb48f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y\\ge {x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e067a2d-a6f5-52ff-b110-b65c2fa82a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312845} :answer-field/id #uuid "af304b58-5892-5cc4-8da6-3654443376de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "626b0475-a4cb-5242-a0c8-33a38a54ece8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161599" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2y\\ge {x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/0fc95ee69ce7121c0cf8ae68029b135d9667f50275227d141f1d979f85408859.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the  parabola $-2y={x}^{2}.$ \n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2y={x}^{2}\\,}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d279c7d-b657-5646-9c60-59c9c5a567c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312852} {:answer/id #uuid "b01d0659-cb8d-569e-9eae-9ff8c0d2ec82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312853} {:answer/id #uuid "0291649b-609c-5efe-afd8-fc4e43c8ac62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312854} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3aa9cc-38a0-5be5-ad04-eedb6acb478e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312855} {:answer/id #uuid "b743fa62-6f63-567c-b90c-2ed0391e9e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b01d0659-cb8d-569e-9eae-9ff8c0d2ec82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312853} :answer-field/id #uuid "927d3a65-73f1-55ef-906d-e986eb1d878c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "157d4457-1f9d-56e1-8728-9f512b3e63e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161622" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -1+x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/3e7d8c11ac4b3de8473741a362ad2b78555c764da2d61f955435e8d59771cf46.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24911b0e-c9d5-5861-b5ff-fd0fcb4ff12f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312859} {:answer/id #uuid "4982bc7f-6393-550c-8019-d27e45da89c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312860} {:answer/id #uuid "8d020131-8ca5-5ac2-93bb-296db0e835a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312861} {:answer/id #uuid "b06acb3c-1b5f-5e4b-acd5-3d88191085f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312862} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb0c4b8-2fb2-5c9c-9594-9a0f3269b936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d020131-8ca5-5ac2-93bb-296db0e835a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312861} :answer-field/id #uuid "61119d38-e53f-549e-8495-e7db2ce3039a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2a4227a-fdad-57d8-886c-1e780898ed55" :question/math-academy-id "q-161625" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge 2-x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/cdca164d343d8bff4c78a1e366e52c588ca6c54dfdea61acea1900bcbdc79f31.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and II are false. As we have seen, $A$ contains all its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c661fcfe-c80e-57aa-985a-513b1f79f913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312866} {:answer/id #uuid "e87ff6e8-d276-588e-8bfd-078bb9d41f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312867} {:answer/id #uuid "b998510f-38fa-54a4-9474-b1cec36cded4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312868} {:answer/id #uuid "de93781e-a6ed-5713-96bf-ffa75c19b73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312869} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e2527a-df6e-53b9-8a50-db65ef333ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c661fcfe-c80e-57aa-985a-513b1f79f913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312866} :answer-field/id #uuid "01680378-49dc-5671-b5de-e62d50e41efd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81147b8a-c890-53be-ba74-33cc71183412" :question/math-academy-id "q-161626" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∂A$\n- $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/4540e03fc5404474a0cd900c89004e6a5cfe7ac2d4702348e9d0395fc1c914e9.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/2a93b912f0d7b3a84bf0ec9c3b3596d080a9e71a345ff436e2f591edf042d9db.png)\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A$ or, equivalently,  $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b89315a-66ee-5cb3-bf1a-b190c5bfc2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312873} {:answer/id #uuid "86f324ac-8393-5a4e-b025-26403f65a974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312874} {:answer/id #uuid "90507c41-1430-51e3-9bbc-9e666ac8cbdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312875} {:answer/id #uuid "55be89ac-4e7e-510a-9264-714c7968d02e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312876} {:answer/id #uuid "2efcda97-048f-51b6-a815-2d871192a1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55be89ac-4e7e-510a-9264-714c7968d02e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312876} :answer-field/id #uuid "0470d837-42ad-533e-912b-a4e9efc38a70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7f2a33f-92c6-54d6-a86e-fbe7b1778994" :question/math-academy-id "q-161643" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4y>{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/a380742f47d97062d56b482592cd2d2961dcc40262131a99886b815c507ab6e3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/26d264d702368ed6fe05577d7c34630bd16b41bdba8d206dd4db39763db02938.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊈A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76b2cfe7-f5e5-5d21-8db4-c909d92e2a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312880} {:answer/id #uuid "61261487-ebbc-5927-9fd8-3eb279c19248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312881} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a05b0d-6604-5fc1-a06b-0bedf50272a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312882} {:answer/id #uuid "8c748f1f-b4fe-5756-935d-5080dd2e4af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312883} {:answer/id #uuid "64710a2c-035f-51cf-9b70-0f5eded45816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3a05b0d-6604-5fc1-a06b-0bedf50272a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312882} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e543fc1-f762-5bf7-8837-d7f1ae4f6c01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d21a66c2-1c83-597c-83d2-92810c6d2822" :question/math-academy-id "q-161700" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,0\\le x\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,,\\,0<x<1}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/d60700d57ddc72bb410b3136f546705676f83d23d9071ffe972c5163d11af549.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/75e0132cd3854f18e9ab409b7fd13af3898f78b5174647bf2b33b8438b9228cb.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,0<x<1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "436b0799-2922-5bfb-b160-42a3535d967c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1\\le y\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312887} {:answer/id #uuid "07412eb7-25dc-5290-b340-11003d610652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312888} {:answer/id #uuid "458c5473-d80c-505a-ab0b-061c6334a76f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1<y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312889} {:answer/id #uuid "036be952-6afa-5005-bb2d-442bdba8e76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312890} {:answer/id #uuid "83919eea-f4c7-50e2-a78a-e9b5363c3f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1\\le y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "458c5473-d80c-505a-ab0b-061c6334a76f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1<y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312889} :answer-field/id #uuid "f152ab5d-27fe-5d70-b906-8a2896051ece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b70db18a-f986-5038-af78-cdfc189b27cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161702" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/84806884f843154c17c338eef0f50d83cae049aece675450e21e58dade6a9f34.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/ce268d8b396ea63227734c1edd6b876bb0fe9755fa12a3b75be90c6ef4af5af3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/84780a84406a3fd0e281737b979887e0de6b1e9fe39d440f379fbb2b84e80a3b.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-1<y<0}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "981e1171-6971-5221-bea1-76d28fe0de8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x<y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312894} {:answer/id #uuid "95fd32f6-8baf-53be-9b17-65a9692681fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le y\\le -x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312895} {:answer/id #uuid "1c19ec69-57c6-5cef-822e-84038e4a1ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<y\\le -x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312896} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9fbe3e-479e-50e9-97a2-58ef802961cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<y<-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312897} {:answer/id #uuid "0790faa2-dbae-5f58-98b1-81e9bdbf30d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\le y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9fbe3e-479e-50e9-97a2-58ef802961cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<y<-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312897} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b97c823-fa34-565c-9499-4695991be6e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7861d2f7-e3b6-5b7c-a05b-2009b500c4e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-71889" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2<x<0,\\,0<y<-x}.\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2<x<0,\\,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing 